<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Town Council – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Town Council</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How We Share Our Voice in the Community</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Compose essays on general life experiences. Interactive: Participate in text-based discussions like messages, email discussions, chats, or online forums.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can compose essays on civic engagement, using examples from the Town Council scenario (e.g., "Citizens must vote to have a say in community decisions."). Can participate in online discussions about civic responsibilities, posting comments about local issues (e.g., "I believe citizens should attend town meetings to share their views."). Can respond to classmates' posts in a discussion forum, adding perspectives on community involvement (e.g., "In my opinion, volunteering improves our town.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 25px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher begins by projecting images of different community issues (e.g., litter in a park, broken public services, a new community project). Students are asked to briefly brainstorm in pairs one or two sentences about what they observe and what might be a good solution. The teacher then asks a few students to share their ideas orally to activate prior knowledge related to community involvement.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to express an opinion on a community issue using target vocabulary and modals, ensuring correct pronunciation, especially for /ɪ/ in "public" and /e/ in "share." For example, the teacher writes on the board: "Citizens <b>must</b> <b>participate</b> in local <b>decisions</b> to improve <b>public services</b>. I believe residents <b>should</b> attend <b>town council</b> meetings." The teacher highlights words like 'public', 'civic', 'community', 'share', 'serve', modeling their pronunciation and practicing word stress in 'decision' and 'participation'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the main goal of sharing your voice in the community?" (Expected answer: To influence decisions, to improve things, to express opinions.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can anyone give an example of a civic responsibility mentioned?" (Expected answer: Voting, attending meetings, volunteering.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short blog post from a fictional "Our Town Council" website titled "Your Voice Matters: Recent Community Feedback and Action." The post discusses an issue (e.g., traffic safety), mentions feedback collected from citizens (passive voice: "Concerns were raised by residents"), reports what a council member said (reported speech: "Councilor Smith stated that new measures would be considered"), and suggests actions citizens "can" or "should" take.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will understand how community feedback is processed and communicated within a civic context, identifying key arguments and proposed actions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main topic of the blog post, and what is its purpose?" (Students write a one-sentence answer.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read 3-4 statements about the blog post and mark them as True or False, correcting the false ones by writing the correct information. (e.g., "The council decided to implement new policies immediately." -> False, "new policies would be considered.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match phrases describing civic responsibilities or actions (e.g., "collect feedback," "prioritize projects") with the corresponding paragraph in the blog post where they are mentioned.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice writing sentences using passive voice, reported speech, and modals ("can," "should," "must," "may," "might") with vocabulary related to "Our Town Council" and "How We Share Our Voice in the Community" (e.g., <b>feedback, decisions, public services, participate, vote, responsibilities</b>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Transformation):</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with sentences in active voice or direct speech related to community issues. Students rewrite them using passive voice, reported speech, or adding appropriate modals.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Examples:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active: "The citizens reported the lack of public services." -> Passive: "The lack of public services <b>was reported</b> by the citizens."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Direct: Mayor said, "We will improve our roads." -> Reported: "The mayor <b>said that</b> they <b>would improve</b> their roads."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Prompt: "It's important to vote." -> Modals: "Citizens <b>must vote</b> to elect good authorities."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice (Guided Sentence Construction):</b> Students are given sentence starters or prompts and must complete them with their own ideas, incorporating target grammar and vocabulary. The focus is on forming grammatically correct sentences that convey civic opinions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Examples:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Many projects _________________ (passive voice) after public feedback." (e.g., "are undertaken")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The community leader informed us that _________________ (reported speech)." (e.g., "new initiatives might be launched.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"To address the issue of sustainability, citizens _________ (modal) _________ (verb)." (e.g., "should contribute to recycling programs.")</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Peer Review of Short Paragraphs):</b> In pairs, students write two short paragraphs (3-4 sentences each) expressing an opinion on a local issue (e.g., "The importance of volunteering" or "Why attending town meetings is essential"). They must use at least one instance of passive voice, one of reported speech, and two modals. They then exchange their paragraphs with their partner, check for correct grammar and vocabulary usage, and provide brief written feedback.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Students will write an argumentative online forum post for the "Our Town Council" community discussion board. This post should be a minimum of 150 words and address a specific local issue (e.g., improving public transportation, increasing green spaces, promoting responsible civic participation). They must express their viewpoint, support it with examples, and suggest actionable solutions. Additionally, students must respond to a hypothetical classmate's post (provided by the teacher) by writing a short comment (50-70 words) that adds perspective or respectfully disagrees, using formal language and relevant vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are presented with a choice of 2-3 local issues they can write about, related to their community (e.g., "How can citizens improve local parks?", "What responsibilities do we have in maintaining public safety?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They plan their main post, considering arguments and solutions. They are explicitly instructed to use at least three instances of passive voice, two instances of reported speech, and several modals (e.g., "Citizens <b>must</b> act," "Improvements <b>can be made</b>," "The authorities <b>might consider</b> new proposals").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students draft their main online forum post individually, focusing on clear expression of ideas and accurate use of target grammar and vocabulary (e.g., <b>community, participation, sustainability, decisions, express, involve, share</b>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After completing their main post, they are given a sample peer post (a short paragraph written by the teacher, presented as a classmate's comment) and tasked with writing a respectful response to it, either supporting or offering an alternative viewpoint. This encourages interactive writing and the use of sociolinguistic functions (e.g., "Thank you for sharing your thoughts on our community," "I respectfully believe that...").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> The teacher will use a rubric focusing on writing quality, grammatical accuracy, and appropriate vocabulary use.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their complete argumentative online forum post and their response to a classmate's post.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Content & Coherence:</b> Clearly states an opinion, provides relevant examples, and offers coherent suggestions for solutions (aligned with learning outcomes: "Can compose essays on civic engagement").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar Accuracy:</b> Correct and appropriate use of passive voice, reported speech, and modals (can, should, must, may, might) (e.g., "Decisions are made based on community feedback," "The mayor said that new policies would be implemented soon," "Citizens must vote").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> Effective and varied use of target vocabulary (e.g., <b>town council, community, voice, feedback, decisions, public services, participation, responsibilities</b>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task Completion & Interaction:</b> Responds appropriately and respectfully to the hypothetical classmate's post, adding perspective (aligned with learning outcome: "Can respond to classmates' posts").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sociolinguistic Appropriacy:</b> Uses formal and respectful language suitable for a civic discussion forum.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new vocabulary word you used in your writing today, and how did it help you express your ideas about civic responsibility?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel using passive voice or reported speech to share information about community decisions?" (Scale of 1-5, with 5 being very confident).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of writing your online forum post, and how did you overcome it?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can you apply what you learned about expressing your voice in the community to real-life situations outside of school?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of the writing task worked well, allowing students to demonstrate their understanding and application of the target grammar and vocabulary?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What specific areas of writing (e.g., complex sentence structure, specific grammar points like reported speech, vocabulary range) need further reinforcement or scaffolding in future lessons?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can the explicit focus on pronunciation of target words (e.g., /ɪ/, /e/ followed by /r/, word stress) be integrated more effectively, even in a writing-focused lesson?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration), students will mediate and integrate the information from their written forum posts, perhaps by summarizing key arguments from their and their peers' posts, or by creating a consolidated recommendation for the Town Council based on the discussion, leading to the 21st Century Skills Project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>